.TH TRAJLAB 2
.SH NAME
trajlab: run, parse \- Danby flight-path models as scripts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "sys.m";
include "draw.m";
include "trajlab.m";
trajlab := load Trajlab Trajlab->PATH;

err := trajlab->run(ctxt, name, spec);
(m, err) := trajlab->parse(spec);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Trajlab
is the Danby trajectory viewer as a library the model calls:
.PP
.EX
trajlab->run(ctxt, "basketball", SPEC);
.EE
.PP
The vocabulary is
.IR dynsys (2)'s
\(em
.BR param ,
.BR maxtime ,
.BR state ,
.BR derivative ,
.B eq
\(em plus
.B title
and
.BR manual .
.PP
The state is fixed at four values, in this order: horizontal position,
height, horizontal velocity, vertical velocity.  The viewer plots the
first two against each other and the speed against time, and stops the
flight when the height crosses zero from above, so the order is not a
convention but a requirement.
.I Trajlab
says so, with an error from
.B run
or
.BR parse ,
rather than letting a mis-ordered spec look plausible on the screen.
.PP
A parameter's slider runs from zero to its maximum, as this family's
.B parameterranges()
always meant.
.PP
The window is Tk's.  The parameter sliders are Tk scales, the buttons are
Tk buttons, the status line is a Tk label, and the layout is a Tk
geometry manager's; the flight path and the speed trace share one
.IR panel (9)
widget holding an image the library draws into.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/trajlab.b
